# Input and Output in Programming

## Difficulty: Basic

---

## Problem Statement

Input and Output (I/O) are fundamental concepts in programming.

* **Input** allows a program to receive data from users, files, or other sources.
* **Output** allows a program to display results or information.
* I/O operations make programs interactive and capable of processing dynamic data.

---

## Concept

Every programming language provides mechanisms to:

1. Accept data from the user (**Input**)
2. Display data to the user (**Output**)

### General Flow

```text
User Input → Program Processing → Output
```

---

## Input and Output in C

### Key Functions

* `printf()` → Displays output.
* `scanf()` → Reads input.
* `\n` → New line character.

### Common Format Specifiers

| Specifier | Data Type |
| --------- | --------- |
| `%d`      | Integer   |
| `%f`      | Float     |
| `%c`      | Character |
| `%s`      | String    |

### Example

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int age;
    float height;
    char grade;
    char name[20];

    scanf("%d %f %c %s", &age, &height, &grade, name);

    printf("Your age: %d\n", age);
    printf("Your height: %.2f\n", height);
    printf("Your grade: %c\n", grade);
    printf("Your name: %s\n", name);

    return 0;
}
```

### Input

```text
20 5.9 A Alice
```

### Output

```text
Your age: 20
Your height: 5.90
Your grade: A
Your name: Alice
```

